<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Quartiles of grouped data</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The table shows the masses, in kg (to the nearest kg), of \({@n@}\) sacks of potatoes delivered to a market in Nyandarua.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Mass (kg)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[2]@}–{@hi[2]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[3]@}–{@hi[3]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[4]@}–{@hi[4]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[5]@}–{@hi[5]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[6]@}–{@hi[6]@}</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (\(f\))</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>For grouped data, the median or a quartile at a given position is estimated by \[ L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c \]where \(L\) is the lower class boundary of the class that holds the position, \(CF\) the cumulative frequency before that class, \(F\) its frequency and \(c\) the class width.</p><p>(a) Find the position of the lower quartile, \(Q_1\).</p><div>Position \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find \(Q_1\), correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div>\(Q_1 =\) [[input:ans2]] kg [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the position of the upper quartile, \(Q_3\).</p><div>Position \(=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Find \(Q_3\), correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div>\(Q_3 =\) [[input:ans4]] kg [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\(Q_1\) has a quarter of the data at or below it. For grouped data the positions are \(n/4\) and \(3n/4\), with no \(+1\), because a position is a point on a continuous scale. Inside a class we assume the values are spread evenly from the lower class boundary to the upper, so we go the right fraction of the way across the class.</p><p>The masses are to the nearest kg, so the class {@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@} starts at the lower class boundary \({@exd(lcb[1])@}\), and so on.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Class</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (\(f\))</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Cumulative frequency (cf)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[1]@}–{@hi[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[1]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[2]@}–{@hi[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[2]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[3]@}–{@hi[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[3]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[4]@}–{@hi[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[4]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[5]@}–{@hi[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[5]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@lo[6]@}–{@hi[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@cf[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>(a) \(n = {@n@}\), so \(Q_1\) is at position \({@n@} \div 4 = {@exd(p1)@}\).</p><p>(b) The first cumulative frequency to reach \({@exd(p1)@}\) is \({@cfq1@}\), so \(Q_1\) is in the class {@lo[k1]@}–{@hi[k1]@}: \(L = {@exd(lcb[k1])@}\), \(CF = {@CF1@}\), \(F = {@F1@}\), \(c = 10\). \[Q_1 = {@exd(lcb[k1])@} + \frac{ {@exd(p1)@} - {@CF1@} }{ {@F1@} } \times 10 \approx {@q1d@}\text{ kg}\]</p><p>(c) \(Q_3\) is at position \(3 \times {@n@} \div 4 = {@exd(p3)@}\).</p><p>(d) The first cumulative frequency to reach \({@exd(p3)@}\) is \({@cfq3@}\), so \(Q_3\) is in the class {@lo[k3]@}–{@hi[k3]@}: \(L = {@exd(lcb[k3])@}\), \(CF = {@CF3@}\), \(F = {@F3@}\). \[Q_3 = {@exd(lcb[k3])@} + \frac{ {@exd(p3)@} - {@CF3@} }{ {@F3@} } \times 10 \approx {@q3d@}\text{ kg}\]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
